ForgeData.registerRetired({"HIST-1":{"label":"Germany 1919–45","color":"#92400e","questions":[{"id":"HIST-01","stem":"A student argues: 'The Weimar Republic was doomed from the start.' Which evidence MOST effectively challenges this interpretation?","options":{"A":"The Stresemann years (1924-29) saw economic recovery, cultural flourishing","B":"The Republic survived the hyperinflation crisis of 1923.","C":"Hitler was democratically elected Chancellor in 1933.","D":"The SPD remained the largest party throughout the Weimar period."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-01","scaffold":"The 'stab in the back' myth, Article 48, proportional representation flaws, and the Depression are all used to argue Weimar was structurally doomed. But the Stresemann era (Golden Twenties) shows the Republic was viable when conditions allowed: Dawes Plan (1924), Locarno Treaties (1925), League of Nations entry (1926), falling unemployment. Contingency (the Depression and its timing) matters as much as structural weaknesses.","reforge":{"stem":"Which of the following MOST supports the view that the Depression, rather than structural weaknesses, caused the Republic's fall?","options":{"A":"The Weimar constitution was written by liberals who didn't understand German politics.","B":"The NSDAP won only 2.6% of the vote in 1928 (before the Depression) but 37.4% in July 1932 — demonstrating that Nazi support surged specifically in response to economic collapse.","C":"Article 48 allowed presidential decrees to bypass the Reichstag.","D":"The SPD refused to work with other parties to form stable coalitions."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"HIST-02","stem":"Which of the following best explains why the Munich Putsch (1923) ultimately benefited Hitler's long-term rise to power?","options":{"A":"It demonstrated that street violence could topple a government.","B":"His trial gave Hitler a national platform","C":"The Putsch united the Nazi party under Hitler's unquestioned leadership.","D":"It convinced Hitler to abandon violence in favour of electoral politics permanently."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-02","scaffold":"The Putsch failed militarily — 16 Nazis died, Hitler was arrested. But: (1) the trial was a media spectacle where Hitler turned proceedings into propaganda, attacking the 'November criminals.' (2) He received only 5 years (served 9 months). (3) Mein Kampf, written in Landsberg Prison, became the ideological foundation. (4) He concluded legal 'legality strategy' was necessary — winning through the ballot box, not the barricades.","reforge":{"stem":"After the Munich Putsch, Hitler pursued what historians call the 'legality strategy.' This means:","options":{"A":"He agreed to abide by the Weimar constitution and abandon all nationalist goals.","B":"He decided to pursue power through elections and legal means rather than direct action — while using propaganda and intimidation alongside, to undermine the system from within.","C":"He formed a legal alliance with the conservative DNVP to enter government.","D":"He moved the Nazi party headquarters to Berlin to improve its electoral organisation."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"HIST-03","stem":"A student writes: 'Hitler became Chancellor because the German people voted for him.' What is the key factual problem with this statement?","options":{"A":"There is no problem — Hitler won the 1933 elections decisively.","B":"Hitler was appointed by the Reichstag rather than the President.","C":"Hitler never won a majority in a free election","D":"Elections were suspended after 1930, so Hitler never appeared on a ballot paper."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-03","scaffold":"Hitler's appointment (January 30, 1933) resulted from elite miscalculation — von Papen convinced Hindenburg they could control Hitler as a 'drummer' for conservative nationalism. Nazi support was falling by late 1932 (party nearly bankrupt). Von Schleicher was dismissed as Chancellor and the conservative 'cabinet of barons' was formed with Hitler nominally in charge. The electoral route to power is partly a myth.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did conservative elites like von Papen support making Hitler Chancellor?","options":{"A":"They genuinely shared Hitler's racial ideology and wanted to implement it.","B":"They believed they could use Hitler's mass appeal to gain popular support while controlling him through a conservative cabinet — fatally underestimating both his ambition and political skill.","C":"They feared another communist revolution if the Nazis were excluded from power.","D":"They were legally required to appoint the leader of the largest party."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"HIST-04","stem":"The Reichstag Fire (February 1933) was significant to Hitler's consolidation of power primarily because:","options":{"A":"It destroyed the Reichstag building and forced the government to rule by emergency decree permanently.","B":"It proved communist terrorism was widespread, permanently discrediting left-wing parties.","C":"It convinced Hindenburg to formally transfer all presidential powers to Hitler.","D":"It provided a pretext for the Reichstag Fire Decree (Article 48)"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-04","scaffold":"Reichstag Fire (27 Feb 1933): Marinus van der Lubbe arrested. Hitler immediately blamed communist conspiracy. Hindenburg signed the Reichstag Fire Decree (28 Feb) — suspended civil liberties (freedom of press, speech, assembly), allowed detention without trial, overrode state governments. ~4,000 communists arrested. The March election (43.9%) with KPD intimidated/banned allowed the Enabling Act to pass.","reforge":{"stem":"Which event directly followed the Reichstag Fire Decree and completed Hitler's legal seizure of dictatorial power?","options":{"A":"The Night of the Long Knives (June 1934), which eliminated the SA leadership.","B":"The Enabling Act (March 1933), which allowed Hitler to pass laws without the Reichstag for four years — making him legislatively independent of parliament.","C":"The banning of all political parties except the NSDAP (July 1933).","D":"Hindenburg's death (August 1934), which allowed Hitler to merge Chancellor and President into the title 'Fuhrer.'"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"HIST-05","stem":"Historians debate whether ordinary Germans who participated in the Holocaust did so primarily due to ideology or situational pressure. Which argument supports the 'situational' interpretation?","options":{"A":"Browning's study of Battalion 101 showed ordinary men, not fanatics, killed under peer pressure.","B":"Germans had been exposed to antisemitic propaganda for decades before 1933.","C":"Nazi racial ideology was explicitly taught in schools from 1933.","D":"The SS selected soldiers for the Einsatzgruppen based on ideological commitment."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-05","scaffold":"Browning ('Ordinary Men'): most Battalion 101 members were not ideologically committed Nazis. When offered the chance to opt out of killing, few did. Explanation: peer pressure, career considerations, obedience to authority, and gradual moral desensitisation. Goldhagen ('Hitler's Willing Executioners') counters: specifically German 'eliminationist antisemitism' was the primary cause. Both interpretations use the same evidence, reaching different conclusions — an excellent example of historical debate.","reforge":{"stem":"Goldhagen's interpretation of Holocaust perpetrators differs from Browning's primarily in arguing that:","options":{"A":"More Germans were involved in the Holocaust than Browning estimated.","B":"German perpetrators were motivated primarily by a specifically German cultural antisemitism ('eliminationist antisemitism') rather than situational factors — they were ideologically driven, not just conforming.","C":"SS ideology rather than ordinary German culture explains perpetrator behaviour.","D":"Economic incentives were the primary motivation for participation in the Holocaust."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"HIST-06","stem":"A student uses only one type of source (e.g. only Nazi propaganda posters) to argue about popular opinion in Germany. What is the key methodological problem?","options":{"A":"Nothing — propaganda posters are the most reliable source for public opinion under any regime.","B":"A single source type only reveals one perspective and needs corroborating with others.","C":"The student should have used only official Nazi party statements instead.","D":"All sources are equally reliable for any historical claim, so source type doesn't matter."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-TECH-01","scaffold":"AQA History source evaluation: a top answer identifies the nature/origin/purpose of the source, considers what it can and cannot tell us, and uses contextual knowledge to corroborate or challenge. Propaganda tells us about regime intent, not necessarily public response. Gestapo reports and SD Meldungen (mood reports) are better (if still imperfect) guides to actual opinion. No single source type is sufficient.","reforge":{"stem":"Which type of source would give a HISTORIAN the most reliable insight into ordinary Germans' actual views of Nazi rule?","options":{"A":"Official Nazi newspaper editorials from the Volkischer Beobachter.","B":"Sicherheitsdienst (SD) opinion reports, which summarised public mood for internal use — written for Nazi leadership, not public consumption, making them more candid than propaganda, though still filtered through party observers.","C":"Post-war memoirs by German civilians who survived the regime.","D":"Photographs of Nazi rallies showing enthusiastic crowds."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"HIST-07","stem":"'The Depression was the most important reason for Hitler's rise to power by 1933.' Write a plan evaluating this claim. (Essay plan — identify and rank factors)","type":"short_answer","model_answer":"Agree: Nazi vote collapsed without Depression context (2.6% in 1928); mass unemployment (6 million by 1932) created fear and desperation that Nazi propaganda exploited; Weimar system appeared incapable. However: Long-term factors also necessary — Weimar structural weaknesses (Article 48, PR system, 'stab in the back' myth), appeal of Nazi ideology (nationalism, antisemitism, strong leadership), and elite miscalculation (von Papen's appointment decision). Most important: Depression was the trigger, not the cause — without the structural weaknesses and Nazi organisation already in place, the Depression alone would not have produced a Nazi government. Judgement: the Depression was necessary but not sufficient — it activated pre-existing structural vulnerabilities.","tag":["MC-HIST-01","MC-TECH-02"],"scaffold":"AQA History essays: a minimum of three factors, each with specific evidence. Top answers rank the factors, explain relationships between them (e.g. how the Depression amplified existing weaknesses), and reach a substantiated judgement. Don't just describe — evaluate which factor was most important AND why the others mattered too."},{"id":"HIST-08","stem":"The Night of the Long Knives (June 1934) consolidated Hitler's power primarily by:","options":{"A":"Eliminating communist opposition to the Nazi regime.","B":"Merging the offices of Chancellor and President into the title 'Fuhrer.'","C":"Destroying the SA leadership (including Rohm), removing a potential rival power base","D":"Purging conservative members of the cabinet who had opposed Nazi policies."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-04","scaffold":"Night of Long Knives (30 June–2 July 1934): ~85 killed including Ernst Rohm (SA leader) and former Chancellor von Schleicher. The SA (2.5 million men) threatened both the regular army (which feared being replaced) and Hitler's authority. By eliminating Rohm, Hitler: removed a rival, gained army loyalty, and demonstrated his willingness to use ruthless violence even against former allies. When Hindenburg died (Aug 1934), the army swore its oath to Hitler personally.","reforge":{"stem":"Why was the army's personal oath of loyalty to Hitler, sworn after Hindenburg's death in August 1934, significant?","options":{"A":"It gave Hitler legal authority to conscript soldiers for a future war.","B":"It bound soldiers personally to Hitler rather than to Germany or the constitution — making organised military resistance to Hitler far more psychologically and legally difficult.","C":"It replaced the Weimar constitution's requirement for civilian control of the military.","D":"It formally merged the SA and regular army under Hitler's supreme command."},"correct":"B"}}]},"HIST-2":{"label":"Cold War & Superpower Relations","color":"#78350f","questions":[{"id":"CW-01","stem":"The Truman Doctrine (1947) was significant primarily because it:","options":{"A":"Committed the USA to providing military and economic aid to countries threatened by communist takeover, marking a shift from isolationism to global containment.","B":"Created NATO as a collective security alliance against Soviet aggression.","C":"Divided Germany into four occupation zones between the Allied powers.","D":"Established the Marshall Plan to rebuild Western European economies."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-06","scaffold":"Truman Doctrine (March 1947): Truman asked Congress for $400m to support Greece and Turkey against communist insurgencies. Crucially, it stated the USA would support 'free peoples who are resisting attempted subjugation.' This was a general commitment to containment worldwide — not just Europe. Distinct from the Marshall Plan (economic aid, June 1947) and NATO (military alliance, 1949).","reforge":{"stem":"How did the Truman Doctrine differ from the Marshall Plan?","options":{"A":"They were identical — both provided military aid to Europe.","B":"The Truman Doctrine was a political commitment to contain communism globally; the Marshall Plan was specifically economic aid to rebuild Western European economies devastated by WWII.","C":"The Marshall Plan was military; the Truman Doctrine was economic.","D":"The Truman Doctrine applied only to Asia; the Marshall Plan to Europe."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"CW-02","stem":"The Berlin Blockade (1948–49) ended because:","options":{"A":"NATO threatened military action against the Soviet Union.","B":"The Western Allies successfully supplied West Berlin by airlift, making the blockade ineffective, and the Soviets lifted it after negotiations.","C":"West Berlin surrendered to Soviet demands.","D":"The USA deployed nuclear weapons to West Germany as a deterrent."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-07","scaffold":"Stalin blockaded all land routes to West Berlin in June 1948 to force the Allies out. The Allied airlift (Operation Vittles) flew over 200,000 flights, delivering 2.3 million tonnes of supplies in 11 months. The blockade became a propaganda victory for the West — Soviet aggression looked worse than Western resolve looked strong. Stalin lifted it in May 1949 having gained nothing. NATO was formed the same month.","reforge":{"stem":"Why was the Berlin Airlift significant beyond its immediate practical purpose?","options":{"A":"It demonstrated that conventional military force was the best response to Soviet pressure.","B":"It was a propaganda victory showing Western resolve and Soviet aggressiveness without risking direct military confrontation — strengthening support for NATO and containment.","C":"It proved the Soviet economy was stronger than the West.","D":"It led directly to German reunification."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"CW-03","stem":"The Cuban Missile Crisis (1962) was resolved primarily through:","options":{"A":"A US military invasion of Cuba that destroyed the missile sites.","B":"UN peacekeeping forces being deployed to Cuba.","C":"Direct negotiation between Kennedy and Khrushchev — the Soviets withdrew missiles in exchange for a US pledge not to invade Cuba and a secret agreement to remove US missiles from Turkey.","D":"Khrushchev backing down unconditionally after Kennedy's naval blockade."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-08","scaffold":"The resolution was a diplomatic compromise, not a US victory: (1) Soviet missiles removed from Cuba; (2) USA publicly pledged not to invade Cuba; (3) USA secretly agreed to remove Jupiter missiles from Turkey within 6 months. Khrushchev could present this as a success domestically (Cuba protected). Kennedy presented it as a US victory publicly. The secret Turkey deal was not revealed for decades. Led directly to the Moscow-Washington hotline.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do some historians argue Khrushchev's position in the Cuban Missile Crisis was stronger than it appeared?","options":{"A":"The Soviet missiles were more powerful than US intelligence estimated.","B":"He secured the removal of US Jupiter missiles from Turkey, protecting Soviet security interests, while appearing to back down publicly — achieving strategic goals without war.","C":"Cuba became a permanent Soviet military base.","D":"The USA was forced to recognise Soviet nuclear superiority."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"CW-04","stem":"Détente in the 1970s is best understood as:","options":{"A":"A period of American military dominance that forced Soviet concessions.","B":"A temporary relaxation of Cold War tensions through diplomacy, arms control agreements (SALT I & II), and trade — while both superpowers continued competing in the developing world.","C":"A permanent end to Cold War rivalry following Soviet economic collapse.","D":"A Chinese-led initiative to mediate between the superpowers."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-09","scaffold":"Détente (French: relaxation): Nixon and Kissinger pursued a more pragmatic foreign policy — playing the 'China card' (Nixon visits China 1972), SALT I (1972) limiting strategic nuclear weapons, Helsinki Accords (1975) recognising post-WWII European borders. BUT competition continued in Africa (Angola), Middle East, and indirectly. Détente collapsed with the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan (1979) and Reagan's hawkish response.","reforge":{"stem":"Which event most clearly signalled the end of détente?","options":{"A":"The Iranian Revolution (1979) — Carter's inability to free the hostages damaged his presidency.","B":"Nixon's resignation following Watergate (1974) — ending détente abruptly.","C":"The Soviet invasion of Afghanistan (1979) — Carter boycotted the Moscow Olympics and withdrew SALT II from Senate ratification, ending détente.","D":"The fall of Saigon (1975) — demonstrating US military weakness to the Soviet Union."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"CW-05","stem":"NSC-68 (1950) recommended that the USA should:","options":{"A":"Dramatically increase defence spending to counter Soviet military expansion and assert American global leadership.","B":"Negotiate directly with the Soviet Union to reduce nuclear stockpiles.","C":"Pursue a policy of rollback rather than mere containment.","D":"Withdraw from European commitments and focus on defending the Western Hemisphere."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-10","scaffold":"NSC-68 (April 1950): top-secret National Security Council report arguing the Soviet threat was existential. Recommended quadrupling US defence spending from $13bn to $50bn annually. Truman had been reluctant to increase military spending, but the Korean War (June 1950) provided the justification. NSC-68 underpinned the militarisation of containment — shifting from Kennan's political/economic containment to military confrontation.","reforge":{"stem":"How did NSC-68 represent a departure from Kennan's original containment strategy?","options":{"A":"It agreed with Kennan that containment should be primarily political and economic.","B":"NSC-68 militarised containment — recommending massive defence spending and treating every Soviet move as a military threat, whereas Kennan envisioned containment as primarily political, economic, and diplomatic.","C":"It proposed negotiating spheres of influence with the Soviets.","D":"It advocated nuclear first-strike capability."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"CW-06","stem":"The policy of 'Mutually Assured Destruction' (MAD) was considered stabilising because:","options":{"A":"It gave the USA clear nuclear superiority over the USSR.","B":"It encouraged both superpowers to develop conventional military forces instead.","C":"It required both sides to seek UN approval before using nuclear weapons.","D":"Both sides understood that any nuclear first strike would trigger a devastating retaliatory response, making nuclear war irrational — deterring both sides from initiating conflict."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-11","scaffold":"MAD logic: if both sides have second-strike capability (can survive a first strike and retaliate), nuclear war becomes suicidal for the aggressor. This 'balance of terror' was paradoxically stabilising — neither side would initiate nuclear war. BUT it was also incredibly fragile: accidental launches, miscalculation (Able Archer 83, Stanislav Petrov incident 1983), or technical failure could trigger catastrophe. Critics argued it was morally bankrupt.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did both superpowers invest heavily in second-strike capability (submarines, dispersed silos) rather than simply building more warheads?","options":{"A":"Submarines were cheaper to build than land-based missiles.","B":"To ensure that even if a first strike destroyed ground-based missiles, enough nuclear weapons would survive to inflict unacceptable retaliatory damage — making a first strike irrational and maintaining deterrence.","C":"International agreements banned land-based missiles.","D":"Submarines could be used for conventional warfare as well."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"CW-07","stem":"The collapse of the Soviet Union (1991) is best explained by which combination of factors?","options":{"A":"Structural economic weaknesses, the cost of the arms race and Afghanistan, Gorbachev's reforms (glasnost and perestroika) which released nationalist and reform pressures the system could not contain, and the demonstration effect of Western prosperity.","B":"Reagan's military build-up alone forced Soviet bankruptcy.","C":"Gorbachev's reforms were the only cause — the USSR was otherwise stable.","D":"The Soviet military staged a coup that ended communist rule."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-12","scaffold":"Multi-causal: (1) Structural — Soviet command economy stagnating, technology gap widening; (2) Military overstretch — arms race cost 15-20% of GDP, Afghanistan (1979-89) was 'Soviet Vietnam'; (3) Gorbachev's reforms — glasnost (openness) and perestroika (restructuring) were intended to save the system but instead unleashed forces that destroyed it; (4) 1989 revolutions in Eastern Europe removed the buffer; (5) August coup (1991) fatally weakened communist authority. No single cause suffices.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did Gorbachev's reforms backfire so fatally?","options":{"A":"Gorbachev was secretly working for the CIA and deliberately undermined the USSR.","B":"Glasnost permitted public criticism of problems that perestroika couldn't fix fast enough — creating a crisis of legitimacy as people saw failures openly but reforms stalled.","C":"Western economic sanctions prevented Gorbachev's reforms from producing results.","D":"The Soviet military directly opposed and sabotaged every reform Gorbachev attempted."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"CW-08","stem":"The Korean War (1950–53) was significant for the Cold War primarily because:","options":{"A":"It resulted in a decisive US victory that pushed communism out of Asia.","B":"It ended with North Korea joining the Western alliance.","C":"It led directly to the formation of NATO.","D":"It was the first major military test of containment and demonstrated that the USA would use force to resist communist expansion, ending in stalemate at the 38th parallel — establishing the pattern of limited war."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-13","scaffold":"Korea: first hot war of the Cold War. North Korea (Soviet and Chinese backed) invaded South Korea (June 1950). UN forces under MacArthur pushed North Korea back; China intervened when forces approached the Yalu River. Ended in armistice (1953) roughly at the original border. Significance: containment proven viable by force; MacArthur fired for wanting to extend the war to China; established 'limited war' doctrine — fighting to prevent communist gains without triggering WWIII.","reforge":{"stem":"Why was Truman's firing of General MacArthur significant?","options":{"A":"MacArthur had lost the Battle for Seoul and Truman dismissed him for incompetence.","B":"It established the principle of civilian control over the military — MacArthur publicly challenged Truman's limited-war strategy, so Truman dismissed him to assert constitutional authority.","C":"MacArthur was found to be passing intelligence to Soviet agents.","D":"His dismissal ended the Korean War immediately by removing the aggressive US commander."},"correct":"B"}}]},"CRIM-1":{"label":"Changing Awareness of Crime","color":"#374151","questions":[{"id":"CRIM-01","stem":"A student defines 'crime' as 'anything that is morally wrong.' Why is this sociologically imprecise?","options":{"A":"Crime is an act that violates criminal law and carries formal state sanctions.","B":"crime and immorality are the same thing.","C":"Crime is defined by victims, not the state.","D":"Morality is irrelevant to criminology."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-CRIM-01","scaffold":"Crime = behaviour that violates criminal law + attracts formal state sanctions. Key points: (1) relative — what counts as crime changes over time (homosexuality was criminalised in the UK until 1967) and across place (cannabis is legal in some US states, illegal in others). (2) Not the same as deviance (violating norms, may be informal) or immorality (violating ethical standards). WJEC expects precise definitional distinctions.","reforge":{"stem":"Which of the following illustrates that crime is a socially constructed concept?","options":{"A":"All societies agree that murder is a crime.","B":"The same act (e.g. possession of cannabis) is a criminal offence in one country but legal and regulated in another — showing that what counts as crime is determined by social and legal context, not by any universal standard.","C":"Crime rates are measured using official statistics.","D":"Criminals are born with genetic predispositions to offend."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"CRIM-02","stem":"What does the 'dark figure of crime' refer to?","options":{"A":"Crime committed during night-time hours that goes unwitnessed.","B":"The dark figure: the gap between actual crime and crime officially recorded.","C":"Crime committed by offenders whose identity is unknown to police.","D":"Violent crime that is especially severe or causes lasting harm."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-CRIM-02","scaffold":"Dark figure = crime that doesn't appear in official statistics because: victim doesn't report (embarrassment, fear, distrust of police), police don't record it (discretion, administrative decisions), no victim to report (drug offences, white-collar crime). This is why official statistics undercount crime. Victim surveys (e.g. Crime Survey for England and Wales) try to measure the dark figure by asking people directly about their experiences.","reforge":{"stem":"Which type of crime is MOST likely to contribute to the dark figure, and why?","options":{"A":"Murder — because bodies are hard to hide.","B":"Domestic abuse — victims often don't report due to fear of the perpetrator, shame, economic dependence, or lack of confidence that the police will help, meaning much of it never enters official statistics.","C":"Speeding — cameras don't catch everyone.","D":"Shoplifting — easy to detect on CCTV."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"CRIM-03","stem":"Which of the following is a limitation of official crime statistics as a measure of crime?","options":{"A":"They include too much data from victim surveys.","B":"They are updated too frequently to be reliable.","C":"They only measure crimes that are reported to and recorded by the police","D":"They overcount crime because police are motivated to record everything."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-CRIM-03","scaffold":"OCS limitations: (1) dark figure — much crime unreported. (2) Police discretion — not all reported crimes are recorded (counting rules, targets). (3) Social construction — what gets counted reflects police priorities, not reality. (4) Exclude victimless crimes. Strengths: consistent methodology over time, allow comparisons, free, cover all recorded crimes. WJEC expects balanced evaluation of sources.","reforge":{"stem":"The Crime Survey for England and Wales (CSEW) is considered to give a more complete picture of crime than official police statistics because:","options":{"A":"It only measures violent crime accurately.","B":"It asks a representative sample of the population directly about crimes they have experienced, including those not reported to the police — capturing part of the dark figure that OCS miss.","C":"It is produced by the government so must be accurate.","D":"It includes data from court convictions."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"CRIM-04","stem":"A criminologist argues that media representations of crime create a 'moral panic.' By this they mean:","options":{"A":"The media accurately reflects the true level and distribution of crime in society.","B":"The public feel morally responsible and guilty when they read about crime.","C":"Crime only increases at times when media coverage of crime intensifies.","D":"The media overrepresents certain crimes, creating exaggerated public fear and distorted perception."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-CRIM-04","scaffold":"Moral panic (Cohen, 1972 — Mods and Rockers): media exaggerates threat → public fear disproportionate to actual risk → demands for action → police/courts respond more harshly → potentially amplifies the problem (deviancy amplification spiral). 'Folk devils' = the group labelled as the threat (e.g. youth gangs, asylum seekers, benefit claimants). Relevant to WJEC Unit 2 on media and crime.","reforge":{"stem":"Following a moral panic about knife crime, police stop-and-search operations increase significantly, leading to more knife-crime arrests. A criminologist argues this data doesn't prove knife crime increased. Why?","options":{"A":"Stop and search is always ineffective.","B":"More searches = more chances to find knives = more arrests. The rise in recorded offences may reflect increased police activity rather than an actual rise in knife crime — this is deviancy amplification, where the response to a moral panic creates evidence that appears to confirm the original panic.","C":"The statistics are deliberately falsified.","D":"Knife crime only occurs in urban areas."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"CRIM-05","stem":"Which of the following best describes 'white-collar crime'?","options":{"A":"Non-violent crime by high-status individuals in the course of their occupation.","B":"Crime committed by people in professional dress or formal business attire.","C":"Any crime that takes place in an office building or corporate environment.","D":"Any crime that does not involve direct physical violence, regardless of status."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-CRIM-05","scaffold":"Sutherland (1949) coined 'white-collar crime' — crime by the powerful in their professional roles. Examples: financial fraud (Bernie Madoff), corporate manslaughter (Grenfell), tax avoidance/evasion, bribery, insider trading. Under-represented in OCS because: complex, harder to detect, powerful offenders have resources to avoid prosecution, treated more leniently. WJEC links this to inequality and selective law enforcement.","reforge":{"stem":"White-collar crime is under-represented in official statistics compared to street crime. Which factor MOST explains this?","options":{"A":"White-collar crime causes less harm than street crime.","B":"White-collar crime is harder to detect, victims may not know they've been defrauded, perpetrators have legal resources to avoid prosecution, and agencies may lack expertise or motivation to pursue powerful offenders — reflecting selective enforcement.","C":"Police deliberately protect rich offenders.","D":"White-collar crime is legal in most cases."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"CRIM-06","stem":"According to strain theory (Merton), crime occurs because:","options":{"A":"Some people are biologically predisposed to criminality.","B":"Merton's strain theory — approved goals with no legitimate means to reach them.","C":"Criminal values are learned through socialisation in communities.","D":"Inadequate policing allows crime to flourish."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-CRIM-06","scaffold":"Merton's strain theory: American Dream = everyone can succeed (meritocracy). Reality = working-class and minority groups lack access to education/jobs. Strain = gap between cultural goals and structural means. Adaptations: Conformity (accept both), Innovation (adopt goals, reject means → crime), Ritualism, Retreatism (reject both), Rebellion. Innovation explains property crime among the poor. Evaluation: ignores white-collar crime, gender, doesn't explain why only some 'innovate'.","reforge":{"stem":"A young person from a deprived area cannot access university due to low income and poor schooling but still wants the material success they see advertised. According to Merton, if they turn to drug dealing, they are adopting which adaptation?","options":{"A":"Ritualism — they keep trying through legitimate means.","B":"Innovation — they accept the cultural goal of material success but replace legitimate means (education, employment) with illegitimate ones (drug dealing) due to blocked opportunities.","C":"Retreatism — they give up on both goals and means.","D":"Rebellion — they reject society's goals entirely."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"CRIM-07","stem":"A student answers a WJEC Criminology question about causes of crime using only one theory. What is the examiner likely to penalise?","options":{"A":"one theory is sufficient for any answer.","B":"The student for using a theory at all.","C":"Lack of range and evaluation","D":"Answers that mention sociological theories."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-TECH-02","scaffold":"WJEC Criminology: assessment requires AO1 (knowledge), AO2 (application), AO3 (analysis/evaluation). Higher bands require considering competing explanations, weighing evidence, and reaching a reasoned conclusion. Single-theory answers are capped. A strong answer might compare strain theory with subcultural theory and biological approaches, noting what each explains and what each fails to account for.","reforge":{"stem":"'Evaluate biological explanations for criminal behaviour.' A top-band answer would:","options":{"A":"Describe Lombroso's theory in great detail.","B":"Explain biological theories (genetics, brain structure, hormones), apply evidence (twin studies, XYY research), then evaluate by comparing with sociological alternatives — noting biological determinism ignores social context and that correlation is not causation.","C":"Argue that biology proves criminals are born, not made.","D":"List all possible causes of crime without evaluating any."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"CRIM-08","stem":"Situational crime prevention (SCP) aims to reduce crime by:","options":{"A":"Rehabilitating offenders through prison education.","B":"Addressing the root social causes of crime such as poverty and inequality.","C":"Increasing police patrol numbers in high-crime areas.","D":"Altering the environment to increase the effort and risk of offending."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-CRIM-08","scaffold":"SCP (Clarke, 1980s) based on rational choice theory — offenders make cost-benefit calculations. Reduce opportunity → reduce crime. Techniques: target hardening (locks, safes), increase surveillance (CCTV), reduce anonymity (name badges), deflect offenders (youth clubs). Evaluation: displacement (crime moves elsewhere, not reduced overall), ignores causes, class-based (protects wealthy areas better). Contrast with social/community crime prevention which addresses underlying causes.","reforge":{"stem":"A criticism of situational crime prevention is that it may cause 'displacement.' By this, critics mean:","options":{"A":"CCTV cameras are moved to other locations.","B":"Crime is not eliminated but merely shifted to other locations, times, or types of offence where prevention measures are weaker — overall levels of crime may not fall.","C":"Offenders are displaced into prison.","D":"Victims move away from high-crime areas."},"correct":"B"}}]},"CRIM-2":{"label":"Criminological Theories","color":"#374151","questions":[{"id":"CRIM-09","stem":"Robert Merton's strain theory argues that crime results from:","options":{"A":"A disconnect between culturally approved goals and the legitimate means available to achieve them.","B":"Biological predispositions that make some individuals naturally deviant.","C":"The absence of formal social control mechanisms in urban environments.","D":"Learned behaviour acquired through association with criminal peers."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-CRIM-06","scaffold":"Merton (1938): American Dream ideology promotes wealth as a goal for everyone, but legitimate means (education, jobs) are unequally distributed. The resulting strain produces 5 adaptations: Conformity (accept both), Innovation (accept goal, reject means — most criminal), Ritualism, Retreatism, Rebellion. Innovators use illegal means (theft, drug dealing) to achieve cultural goals. Explains working-class crime but not white-collar crime.","reforge":{"stem":"Which of Merton's adaptations best describes a person who rejects both cultural goals and legitimate means, withdrawing entirely from society?","options":{"A":"Ritualism — going through the motions without believing in success.","B":"Conformity — accepting both goals and means.","C":"Retreatism — rejecting both goals and means, withdrawing (e.g. chronic drug users, homeless).","D":"Rebellion — replacing goals and means with alternatives."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"CRIM-10","stem":"Social learning theory (Sutherland's Differential Association) explains crime as:","options":{"A":"An innate trait that is genetically inherited.","B":"A result of rational cost-benefit calculation.","C":"Learned through interaction with others, particularly those who hold favourable attitudes toward law violation.","D":"Caused by anomie when social norms break down during rapid social change."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-CRIM-07","scaffold":"Sutherland (1939): crime is learned through communication within intimate groups. If a person is exposed to more definitions favourable to crime than unfavourable, they are likely to offend. Frequency, duration, priority, and intensity of association all matter. This challenges biological and psychological theories — no one is born criminal; crime is a learned behaviour like any other. Supported by evidence on gang membership and peer influence.","reforge":{"stem":"Which finding would most challenge Differential Association theory?","options":{"A":"Children raised in high-crime neighbourhoods are more likely to offend.","B":"Peer groups influence attitudes to law-breaking.","C":"Identical twins raised apart show similar rates of offending, suggesting a genetic component that learning theory cannot explain.","D":"First-time offenders often know others who have committed crimes."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"CRIM-11","stem":"Left Realism (Lea and Young) identifies the 'square of crime'. Which four elements does it include?","options":{"A":"Offender, victim, police, and media.","B":"Poverty, unemployment, inequality, and social exclusion.","C":"Police, courts, prisons, and probation services.","D":"Victim, offender, police, and public."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-CRIM-08","scaffold":"Lea and Young's square of crime: the four elements that must interact to produce crime — (1) offender, (2) victim, (3) police/criminal justice system, (4) public. Left Realists focus on working-class crime and its real impact on working-class communities (unlike Marxists who focus on corporate crime). They advocate for better policing that is accountable to local communities and social reforms to reduce relative deprivation.","reforge":{"stem":"Left Realists criticise both traditional Marxism and Right Realism for taking crime seriously. Why?","options":{"A":"Because crime does not really exist — it is a social construction.","B":"Marxists focus too much on corporate crime and ignore street crime affecting working-class victims; Right Realists blame the individual and ignore structural causes. Left Realists argue both matter.","C":"Because only white-collar crime causes real harm.","D":"Because they both ignore the role of biological factors."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"CRIM-12","stem":"Labelling theory (Becker) argues that deviance is:","options":{"A":"An objective quality that inheres in the act itself.","B":"Genetically determined and therefore fixed.","C":"Not a quality of the act but of the application of rules and sanctions by others — created by social reaction.","D":"Primarily caused by economic inequality."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-CRIM-09","scaffold":"Becker (1963) 'Outsiders': 'deviance is not a quality of the act a person commits, but rather a consequence of the application by others of rules and sanctions.' The same act may be labelled deviant or not depending on who does it, to whom, and who observes it. Labels trigger a 'master status' (deviant above all else), which can lead to a self-fulfilling prophecy and secondary deviance (Lemert). Explains why official statistics over-represent the poor and ethnic minorities.","reforge":{"stem":"Lemert distinguished 'primary deviance' from 'secondary deviance'. Secondary deviance refers to:","options":{"A":"Minor rule-breaking that goes unnoticed.","B":"Deviance committed by a second person copying the first offender.","C":"Deviance that results from internalising the deviant label — the person reorganises their identity around being deviant after societal reaction.","D":"Crime committed in a secondary location away from home."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"CRIM-13","stem":"Feminists critique traditional criminology for:","options":{"A":"Focusing too much on female offenders at the expense of male crime.","B":"Ignoring biological explanations of crime.","C":"Constructing theories based almost entirely on male subjects and then applying them universally, rendering women invisible.","D":"Over-emphasising the role of poverty in causing crime."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-CRIM-10","scaffold":"Feminist criminology (Smart, Heidensohn): traditional criminology was 'malestream' — built on male samples, male researchers, male subjects. Women were either ignored or, when included (e.g. Lombroso), explained through sexist stereotypes. Heidensohn argues women's low crime rates reflect social control (patriarchal expectations, domestic roles, chivalry factor in CJS). Smart argues women who do offend are doubly condemned — for the crime and for violating gender norms.","reforge":{"stem":"The 'chivalry thesis' (Pollak) argues the CJS treats women more leniently. Which evidence challenges this?","options":{"A":"Women are less likely to be arrested than men for equivalent offences.","B":"Women who commit violent crimes are sometimes treated more harshly than men because they violate gender expectations — doubly deviant — suggesting chivalry is not universal.","C":"Female prison populations are smaller than male populations.","D":"Women receive shorter sentences on average."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"CRIM-14","stem":"Right Realism (Wilson and Herrnstein) explains crime primarily through:","options":{"A":"Structural inequality and relative deprivation.","B":"Rational choice combined with individual traits such as low self-control, impulsiveness, and low intelligence.","C":"Labelling by the criminal justice system creating criminal careers.","D":"The collapse of community bonds caused by rapid industrialisation."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-CRIM-11","scaffold":"Wilson and Herrnstein (1985): crime is the result of individual rational choice — people weigh costs and benefits. But choice is shaped by individual traits: low IQ, impulsiveness, low self-control (Gottfredson and Hirschi), and biology. Policy implication: situational crime prevention (make crime harder/riskier) and 'broken windows' policing (Wilson and Kelling). Critics: ignores structural causes, over-represents working-class crime, ignores white-collar crime.","reforge":{"stem":"Zero-tolerance policing, associated with Right Realism, was implemented in New York in the 1990s. Which is a valid critique?","options":{"A":"It reduced crime too effectively, leaving police with nothing to do.","B":"It increased police funding unnecessarily.","C":"It disproportionately targeted young Black and Latino men, raising questions about structural discrimination that Right Realism ignores.","D":"It focused too much on corporate crime."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"CRIM-15","stem":"Hirschi's social bond theory argues that people conform (rather than asking why they offend) because of four bonds. Which is NOT one of them?","options":{"A":"Attachment to others.","B":"Commitment to conventional activities.","C":"Involvement in community organisations.","D":"Genetic predisposition to conformity."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-CRIM-12","scaffold":"Hirschi (1969) — Control Theory: asks why most people don't offend, not why some do. Four bonds to conventional society: (1) Attachment — caring what others think; (2) Commitment — investment in conventional goals (job, qualifications); (3) Involvement — time spent in legitimate activities (less time for crime); (4) Belief — internalised moral norms. Genetic predisposition is a biological explanation, not part of Hirschi's social bond theory.","reforge":{"stem":"A teenager drops out of school, loses their job, and spends time with peers who offend. How does social bond theory explain increased offending risk?","options":{"A":"They have developed a biological predisposition to crime.","B":"They have been labelled deviant by teachers.","C":"Three bonds have weakened — commitment (no qualifications to protect), involvement (more free time), and attachment (peer group normalises crime).","D":"Merton's strain theory better explains this than social bond theory."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"CRIM-16","stem":"Which theoretical perspective would most likely argue that corporate crime is a more serious social problem than street crime?","options":{"A":"Right Realism","B":"Biological positivism","C":"Traditional Marxism","D":"Rational choice theory"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-CRIM-13","scaffold":"Traditional Marxism (Chambliss, Box): the law serves ruling-class interests. Corporate and white-collar crime (tax evasion, health and safety violations, environmental damage) causes more harm — financial, physical, and social — than street crime, but is under-policed, under-prosecuted, and under-punished. The CJS focuses on working-class crime to control the proletariat and protect capitalist interests. Right Realists focus on individual street crime; rational choice theorists see all crime as a cost-benefit calculation regardless of class.","reforge":{"stem":"Marxists argue official crime statistics are ideologically constructed. What do they mean?","options":{"A":"Police make mathematical errors when counting crime.","B":"Statistics reflect media sensationalism rather than real patterns.","C":"The selective enforcement and recording of crime reflects ruling-class power — working-class crime is over-represented because the law and CJS serve to protect capitalist interests and criminalise the powerless.","D":"Statistics are accurate but only reported annually."},"correct":"C"}}]},"LAW-1":{"label":"Criminal Law","color":"#1e3a5f","questions":[{"id":"LAW-01","stem":"The Latin term 'actus reus' refers to:","options":{"A":"The guilty mind — the mental element of a crime.","B":"The standard of proof required in criminal cases.","C":"The defendant's right to remain silent.","D":"The guilty act"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-LAW-01","scaffold":"Every crime requires two elements: actus reus (the guilty act) + mens rea (the guilty mind). Actus reus can be: (1) a positive act (stabbing), (2) an omission where there is a legal duty to act (Airedale NHS Trust v Bland), (3) a state of affairs (Winzar v Chief Constable of Kent — being drunk on a highway). Both elements must coincide in time for most crimes.","reforge":{"stem":"D, a nurse, fails to give a patient medication, knowing they will die without it. The patient dies. The nurse may have committed a criminal act because:","options":{"A":"Omissions are always criminal regardless of context.","B":"There is a duty of care arising from the nurse's professional relationship with the patient, and deliberately failing to act in breach of that duty can constitute a criminal omission.","C":"The nurse acted with intention, which is sufficient for any crime.","D":"A nurse's failure to give medication is automatically manslaughter."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"LAW-02","stem":"In criminal law, 'intention' as a form of mens rea is best defined as:","options":{"A":"Either directly desiring a consequence (direct intent) or foreseeing it as virtually certa","B":"Knowing that harm might result from your actions.","C":"Acting recklessly with no care for the outcome of your conduct.","D":"Making a deliberate decision to ignore a foreseeable risk."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-LAW-02","scaffold":"Direct intent: the defendant desires the consequence (aims to kill). Oblique intent (Woollin [1998]): the consequence is not the primary aim, but the defendant foresaw it as virtually certain — the jury may (not must) find intent. Example: D blows up a plane to claim insurance money; D doesn't want the passengers to die but foresees their death as virtually certain. Contrast with recklessness: subjective Cunningham recklessness (saw the risk and took it unreasonably).","reforge":{"stem":"D sets fire to a building intending to collect insurance money. People inside die. D says he didn't intend to kill anyone. Applying R v Woollin, a jury could find intent to kill because:","options":{"A":"D knew the building was insured, showing premeditation.","B":"D foresaw the deaths of the occupants as a virtually certain consequence of his actions — satisfying the test for oblique intent even though death was not his primary purpose.","C":"Arson is a crime of strict liability so intent is irrelevant.","D":"D acted with recklessness, which is equivalent to intention."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"LAW-03","stem":"Which of the following correctly states the standard of proof in a criminal trial?","options":{"A":"The defendant must prove their innocence on the balance of probabilities.","B":"The prosecution must prove the defendant's guilt beyond reasonable doubt","C":"Both prosecution and defence must prove their cases on the balance of probabilities.","D":"The judge decides guilt based on the weight of evidence without any fixed standard."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-LAW-03","scaffold":"Criminal standard: beyond reasonable doubt (prosecution must reach this — very high). Civil standard: balance of probabilities (more likely than not — 51%). The criminal standard is higher because: liberty is at stake, criminal conviction carries stigma, the state has greater investigative resources than defendants. Exceptions: some defences (insanity, diminished responsibility) must be proved by the defendant on the balance of probabilities.","reforge":{"stem":"D raises the defence of insanity. Under M'Naghten rules, who must prove what and to what standard?","options":{"A":"The prosecution must disprove insanity beyond reasonable doubt.","B":"The defendant must prove the insanity defence on the balance of probabilities — this is an exception to the general rule that defendants need not prove anything.","C":"The jury decides insanity without any formal burden of proof.","D":"The judge determines insanity based on psychiatric evidence alone."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"LAW-04","stem":"For murder, the required mens rea is:","options":{"A":"Recklessness as to whether death or serious injury results.","B":"Knowledge that the victim would probably die as a result of the act.","C":"An intention to kill or cause grievous bodily harm (GBH)","D":"Malice aforethought, meaning the defendant planned the killing in advance."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-LAW-04","scaffold":"Murder mens rea = 'malice aforethought' — intention to kill OR intention to cause GBH (Vickers; Cunningham 1982). 'Malice aforethought' does NOT require premeditation (planning in advance) — intention formed in a split second is sufficient. An intention to cause GBH that leads to death is still murder even if the defendant did not intend to kill. This is why the law of murder is criticised as too wide.","reforge":{"stem":"D punches V intending only to cause serious harm, not death. V dies from the blow. D is charged with murder. Which statement correctly applies the law?","options":{"A":"D cannot be guilty of murder since he did not intend to kill.","B":"D can be guilty of murder because the mens rea includes intention to cause GBH — and that intention was present even though death was not desired.","C":"D is guilty of manslaughter because intent to kill is required for murder.","D":"D is guilty of attempted murder since death was not the intended outcome."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"LAW-05","stem":"The defence of loss of control (Coroners and Justice Act 2009) replaced provocation. Which of the following is a key difference?","options":{"A":"Loss of control requires premeditation; provocation did not.","B":"Loss of control is a complete defence; provocation reduced murder to manslaughter.","C":"Loss of control can be used as a defence to all crimes; provocation was limited to murder.","D":"Loss of control requires a 'qualifying trigger'"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-LAW-05","scaffold":"Provocation (common law): sudden and temporary loss of control caused by provoking acts — R v Duffy. Replaced by loss of control (s.54-55 CJA 2009): (1) D lost self-control, (2) from a qualifying trigger (fear of serious violence OR grave circumstances + justifiable sense of wrong), (3) a person of D's sex and age with normal tolerance would have done the same. Sexual infidelity CANNOT be a qualifying trigger.","reforge":{"stem":"D kills her abusive partner after years of abuse. She acted calmly, having planned the act. Under the 2009 Act, loss of control is unlikely to succeed because:","options":{"A":"Abuse cannot constitute a qualifying trigger under any circumstances.","B":"The defence requires an actual loss of self-control at the time of the killing — acting in a calm, considered way undermines this requirement, though diminished responsibility might be available instead.","C":"The defence can only be used by male defendants.","D":"Planning the act means the killing is premeditated murder with no available defence."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"LAW-06","stem":"A student writes: 'Self-defence is a complete defence to murder because it justifies the killing.' What needs to be added for a precise legal answer?","options":{"A":"The force used must be reasonable and necessary in the circumstances as D believed them to be","B":"Nothing — that is a complete and accurate statement.","C":"Self-defence is only a partial defence, reducing murder to manslaughter.","D":"Self-defence requires the defendant to have first tried to retreat before using force."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-LAW-06","scaffold":"Self-defence / defence of another (s.76 Criminal Justice and Immigration Act 2008): (1) D must genuinely believe force was necessary. (2) Force must be reasonable and proportionate (judged on D's honest belief — R v Williams). (3) Householder cases: force must not be grossly disproportionate. R v Clegg: soldier fired after threat had passed → excessive force → murder conviction. Genuine mistake about need for force: D judged on facts as honestly believed.","reforge":{"stem":"D uses lethal force against a burglar who is fleeing from his home. A court would consider the self-defence claim invalid primarily because:","options":{"A":"Self-defence cannot be used in a home under any circumstances.","B":"The threat had passed once the burglar was fleeing — force used must be necessary and proportionate to the threat at the time it is used, and using lethal force against someone running away is likely excessive.","C":"D should have called the police rather than acting himself.","D":"Lethal force is never permitted against property crimes."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"LAW-07","stem":"For the crime of theft (Theft Act 1968, s.1), which of the following is the correct actus reus?","options":{"A":"Dishonestly obtaining property by deception.","B":"Appropriation of property belonging to another.","C":"Taking property with intent to permanently deprive without consent.","D":"Removing property from its original location without authorisation."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-LAW-07","scaffold":"Theft: s.1 Theft Act 1968 = actus reus + mens rea. Actus reus: (1) Appropriation (s.3 — assuming any right of the owner — R v Gomez: even authorised acts can be appropriation). (2) Property (s.4 — includes money, real property, things in action; excludes confidential information — Oxford v Moss). (3) Belonging to another (s.5 — includes property in possession/control of another). Mens rea: (4) Dishonesty (s.2 — Ivey v Genting Casinos 2017 test). (5) Intention to permanently deprive (s.6).","reforge":{"stem":"D takes a library book intending to return it next week. D has not committed theft because:","options":{"A":"Library books are excluded from the definition of property under the Theft Act.","B":"Theft requires intention to permanently deprive — D intends to return the book, so this element of the mens rea is absent, even if D's appropriation was dishonest.","C":"Taking from a public institution cannot constitute theft.","D":"D had implied permission to take the book by holding a library card."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"LAW-08","stem":"Evaluate the extent to which the law on non-fatal offences against the person achieves justice. (Essay plan — 15 marks)","type":"short_answer","model_answer":"Problems with current law: (1) Statutory offences (Offences Against the Person Act 1861) use outdated, unclear language — 'malicious wounding' (s.20), 'grievous' (no statutory definition). (2) Sentencing anomalies: s.47 ABH (max 5 years) has same maximum as s.20 GBH despite s.20 being more serious. (3) s.39 assault and battery are common law, inconsistent with statutory offences. (4) Law Commission has recommended reform since 1993 — a draft bill (1998) proposed rationalised offences but not enacted. Arguments for justice: the hierarchy of offences (s.39 → s.47 → s.20 → s.18) roughly matches increasing seriousness; CPS charging standards; courts can use sentencing guidelines. Conclusion: the law fails to achieve full justice — the outdated language creates uncertainty, the sentencing structure has anomalies, and parliamentary inaction despite clear reform recommendations reflects institutional failure.","tag":["MC-LAW-01","MC-TECH-02"],"scaffold":"OCR Law essays: AO1 (accurate statement of law with cases), AO2 (application — how the law operates in practice), AO3 (evaluation — does it achieve justice? Law Commission reform?). Top answers reference specific reform proposals and reach a clear judgement."}]},"LAW-2":{"label":"Tort Law","color":"#1e40af","questions":[{"id":"TORT-01","stem":"In Donoghue v Stevenson [1932], Lord Atkin established the 'neighbour principle'. Which statement best captures its legal significance?","options":{"A":"It created a duty of care between manufacturers and the ultimate consumer, establishing the modern law of negligence.","B":"It held that manufacturers owe a duty only to the retailers who purchase their products.","C":"It introduced the concept of contributory negligence as a partial defence.","D":"It limited liability in tort to cases where a contract existed between the parties."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-LAW-08","scaffold":"Donoghue v Stevenson: Mrs Donoghue found a decomposed snail in ginger beer. No contract with the manufacturer (she didn't buy it). Lord Atkin's 'neighbour principle' — you must take reasonable care to avoid acts or omissions that you can reasonably foresee would injure your neighbour (anyone closely and directly affected). This established negligence as an independent tort, no contract required.","reforge":{"stem":"Why was it significant that Mrs Donoghue had no contract with the manufacturer?","options":{"A":"It meant she could not bring any legal action at all.","B":"Contract law did not apply, so a new tortious duty was needed — showing tort operates independently of contract.","C":"She had to rely on criminal law instead.","D":"It proved the manufacturer was not liable."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TORT-02","stem":"The three-stage Caparo test for establishing a duty of care requires which elements?","options":{"A":"Proximity, causation, and remoteness of damage.","B":"Intention, foreseeability, and breach.","C":"Foreseeability of harm, proximity of relationship, and that it is fair, just and reasonable to impose a duty.","D":"Foreseeability of harm, voluntary assumption of risk, and consent."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-LAW-09","scaffold":"Caparo Industries v Dickman [1990] — three-stage test: (1) Was the damage reasonably foreseeable? (2) Was there sufficient proximity between claimant and defendant? (3) Is it fair, just and reasonable to impose a duty? All three must be satisfied. Students confuse this with the elements of breach (the reasonable person standard) — that's a separate question.","reforge":{"stem":"A financial adviser gives negligent advice to a stranger at a party. Why might stage 3 of Caparo prevent a duty arising?","options":{"A":"Financial loss can never be claimed in tort.","B":"There was no proximity between the parties.","C":"It may not be fair, just and reasonable to impose liability for informal social advice — courts are reluctant to extend duty of care too widely.","D":"The advice was not foreseeable."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TORT-03","stem":"The standard of care in negligence is judged by reference to:","options":{"A":"What the defendant believed was reasonable given their personal experience.","B":"The standard of a reasonable person in the defendant's position.","C":"The highest possible standard of professional conduct available.","D":"What the claimant expected the defendant to do."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-LAW-10","scaffold":"The objective 'reasonable person' standard — Blyth v Birmingham Waterworks (1856). Not what this defendant thought was reasonable (subjective), but what a reasonable person in the same circumstances would have done. For professionals, the Bolam test applies: the standard of a reasonable professional in that field — but still objective, not the highest possible standard.","reforge":{"stem":"A trainee doctor makes an error that an experienced consultant would not have made. Are they judged by the trainee's standard?","options":{"A":"Yes — the law makes allowances for inexperience.","B":"No — they are judged by the standard of a reasonably competent doctor in that post, not a junior. The role, not the individual's experience, sets the standard.","C":"Only if the patient knew they were a trainee.","D":"Yes, but damages are reduced proportionally."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TORT-04","stem":"Which case established the 'eggshell skull' (thin skull) rule in tort law?","options":{"A":"Paris v Stepney Borough Council [1951]","B":"Haley v London Electricity Board [1965]","C":"Smith v Leech Brain & Co [1962]","D":"Wagon Mound No 1 [1961]"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-LAW-11","scaffold":"Smith v Leech Brain: a splash of molten metal burned a worker's lip; he had a pre-existing condition that caused cancer and he died. The burn itself was foreseeable — the cancer and death were not. Rule: you take your victim as you find them. If the type of harm was foreseeable, the defendant is liable for its full extent even if the severity was not foreseeable due to the claimant's pre-existing vulnerability.","reforge":{"stem":"A defendant negligently scratches a claimant who, unknown to the defendant, has a rare bleeding disorder and nearly dies. Applying the thin skull rule:","options":{"A":"The defendant is only liable for a minor scratch — the rest was not foreseeable.","B":"The defendant is liable for all consequences if the type of harm (physical injury) was foreseeable, even though the severity was not.","C":"Liability is shared equally between the defendant and the claimant's doctor.","D":"There is no liability because the outcome was unforeseeable."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TORT-05","stem":"Remoteness of damage in negligence is governed primarily by which case?","options":{"A":"Donoghue v Stevenson [1932]","B":"Wagon Mound No 1 [1961]","C":"Caparo v Dickman [1990]","D":"Bolton v Stone [1951]"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-LAW-12","scaffold":"The Wagon Mound (No 1): oil spilled into Sydney Harbour caught fire from welding sparks, destroying ships. The Privy Council held you are only liable for damage of a type that was reasonably foreseeable — not all direct consequences (overruling the old Re Polemis direct consequence test). Key distinction: the type of damage must be foreseeable, even if its exact extent is not (thin skull rule still applies).","reforge":{"stem":"A defendant negligently spills chemicals. The chemicals react unexpectedly with soil to cause a rare explosion. Applying Wagon Mound remoteness:","options":{"A":"The defendant is liable because they caused the spill.","B":"The defendant is only liable if an explosion of this type was a reasonably foreseeable consequence of the spill.","C":"The defendant is liable for all consequences of their negligence.","D":"The defendant is not liable because no harm was intended."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TORT-06","stem":"Volenti non fit injuria as a defence in negligence means:","options":{"A":"The claimant contributed to their own harm.","B":"The defendant acted on the instructions of a third party.","C":"The claimant voluntarily accepted the risk of harm, providing a complete defence.","D":"The damage was too remote to be recoverable."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-LAW-13","scaffold":"Volenti: a complete defence — if the claimant fully and freely consented to the legal risk of harm, the defendant owes no duty. Hard to establish: consent must be free and informed (not under economic pressure). Distinguished from contributory negligence (partial — reduces damages proportionally). Classic case: Dann v Hamilton [1939] — claimant knew driver was drunk but got in anyway; court held volenti did not apply because she didn't fully accept the legal risk.","reforge":{"stem":"How does volenti differ from contributory negligence?","options":{"A":"They are the same defence with different names.","B":"Volenti is a complete defence extinguishing liability entirely; contributory negligence is partial and reduces damages by the claimant's percentage of fault.","C":"Contributory negligence is a complete defence; volenti only reduces damages.","D":"Volenti applies only in criminal law."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TORT-07","stem":"The Occupiers' Liability Act 1957 imposes a 'common duty of care' on occupiers towards:","options":{"A":"All persons who enter the premises, regardless of how they enter.","B":"Lawful visitors only.","C":"Trespassers and lawful visitors equally.","D":"Only paying customers who have a contract with the occupier."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-LAW-14","scaffold":"OLA 1957: duty owed to lawful visitors — those with express or implied permission to enter (invitees, licensees). The Occupiers' Liability Act 1984 separately governs the lower duty owed to trespassers. Students confuse the two Acts. 1957 = visitors. 1984 = trespassers. Under 1957, the standard is to take such care as is reasonable to see the visitor will be reasonably safe for the purpose of their visit.","reforge":{"stem":"A child trespasses onto a farm and is injured by unfenced machinery. Which Act is most relevant?","options":{"A":"Occupiers' Liability Act 1957 — all entrants are covered.","B":"Occupiers' Liability Act 1984 — this Act governs the duty owed to trespassers.","C":"The law of contract, since the farmer impliedly invited the child.","D":"Neither Act — trespassers have no legal protection."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TORT-08","stem":"In psychiatric injury cases, a 'secondary victim' must satisfy the Alcock control mechanisms. Which is NOT one of them?","options":{"A":"Proximity of relationship to the primary victim.","B":"Proximity to the accident in time and space.","C":"Perception of the accident through unaided senses.","D":"Proof that the defendant intended to cause psychiatric harm."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-LAW-15","scaffold":"Alcock v Chief Constable of South Yorkshire [1991] — Hillsborough disaster. Control mechanisms for secondary victims: (1) close tie of love and affection with primary victim; (2) present at the scene or its immediate aftermath; (3) perceived events with own unaided senses (not TV). These are policy controls to limit floodgates. Intention is irrelevant — negligence is a fault-based tort, not intention-based.","reforge":{"stem":"A claimant watches a disaster unfold live on TV and suffers PTSD. Can they recover as a secondary victim?","options":{"A":"Yes — foreseeability is all that matters.","B":"Yes, if they have a close relationship with a primary victim.","C":"No — secondary victims must perceive events through unaided senses; TV broadcasts do not satisfy the Alcock control mechanism.","D":"Yes, if the broadcaster negligently showed graphic footage."},"correct":"C"}}]},"POL-1":{"label":"Paper 1: UK Politics & Ideologies","color":"#1e3a5f","questions":[{"id":"POL-01","stem":"A student describes the UK constitution as 'written in a single document.' Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"It is correct — the UK constitution is codified.","B":"The UK constitution is entirely unwritten.","C":"The UK has an uncodified constitution","D":"Constitutions are only found in republican states."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-POL-01","scaffold":"Uncodified (not unwritten): key sources include statute law (Parliament Acts 1911/49, Human Rights Act 1998, Fixed-term Parliaments Act 2011), constitutional conventions (collective cabinet responsibility, Salisbury convention), common law, royal prerogative, and authoritative texts (Erskine May, Bagehot's 'The English Constitution'). Unlike the US (1787) or Germany (1949), no single document contains all constitutional principles.","reforge":{"stem":"Which of the following is an example of a constitutional convention rather than a statute in the UK?","options":{"A":"The Human Rights Act 1998","B":"The principle that the Prime Minister must command a majority in the House of Commons and must resign if they lose a vote of no confidence.","C":"The Parliament Act 1911","D":"The Fixed-term Parliaments Act 2011"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"POL-02","stem":"First-past-the-post (FPTP) elections are criticised primarily for:","options":{"A":"Producing too many small parties and unstable coalitions.","B":"Being too slow to produce a result.","C":"Allowing too much voter choice.","D":"Disproportionality"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-POL-02","scaffold":"FPTP weaknesses: disproportional outcomes (2019: Conservatives won 56% of seats with 44% of votes), wasted votes (all votes beyond plurality in each constituency), safe seats reduce competition, two-party squeeze (smaller parties dramatically under-represented), tactical voting distorts true preferences. AV (2011 referendum rejected), PR systems avoid many of these. Edexcel questions often ask you to evaluate FPTP against specific alternatives.","reforge":{"stem":"In the 2015 UK general election, UKIP won 12.6% of the national vote but only 1 seat. The SNP won 4.7% of the vote but 56 seats. Which feature of FPTP explains this?","options":{"A":"UKIP's votes were invalid due to party registration errors.","B":"FPTP rewards concentration of votes in constituencies — UKIP votes were spread nationally (never a plurality anywhere); SNP votes were concentrated in Scottish seats where they won pluralities.","C":"Proportional representation applied to UKIP but not the SNP.","D":"Only registered parties can win multiple seats under FPTP."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"POL-03","stem":"Parliamentary sovereignty means that:","options":{"A":"Parliament is the supreme legal authority in the UK","B":"The monarch has ultimate authority over Parliament.","C":"The Prime Minister controls all parliamentary business.","D":"The Supreme Court can strike down Acts of Parliament."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-POL-03","scaffold":"Dicey's three principles of parliamentary sovereignty: Parliament can legislate on anything; no Parliament can bind its successors; no body can override Parliament. Challenges: EU membership (1972–2020, EU law supremacy — Factortame case), devolution (created powerful sub-national bodies), Human Rights Act (courts issue declarations of incompatibility — but Parliament need not act), referendums (political but not legally binding). Brexit reasserted parliamentary sovereignty.","reforge":{"stem":"In the Factortame case (1990), UK courts effectively set aside an Act of Parliament. This is significant because:","options":{"A":"It proved the Supreme Court can overrule Parliament permanently.","B":"It showed that while EU membership lasted, EU law took precedence over Acts of Parliament — a practical qualification to parliamentary sovereignty that Brexit subsequently removed.","C":"It established that conventions are legally superior to statutes.","D":"It transferred power from Parliament to the judiciary permanently."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"POL-04","stem":"A student writes that the House of Lords has 'the same legislative power as the House of Commons.' Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"It is correct — both chambers are equal.","B":"The Lords is subordinate to the Commons","C":"The Lords has more power because it is unelected.","D":"The Lords was abolished in 1999."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-POL-04","scaffold":"Lords powers: revise and delay legislation (up to 1 year under Parliament Acts), amend bills, provide scrutiny, use ping-pong. Lords CANNOT: veto bills indefinitely, delay money bills, reject manifesto commitments (Salisbury Convention). Lords abolished most hereditary peers (1999 reform) — now life peers dominate. Lords reform remains incomplete — an elected chamber has been repeatedly proposed but not implemented.","reforge":{"stem":"The House of Lords rejected a government bill not included in the governing party's manifesto. This is constitutionally significant because:","options":{"A":"The Lords cannot reject any bill under the Parliament Acts.","B":"The Salisbury Convention does not apply to non-manifesto bills — the Lords may legitimately use its delaying power to force the government to reconsider, as it is not bound to pass what was not in the manifesto.","C":"Only the House of Commons can reject government bills.","D":"The Lords' veto is absolute for non-manifesto bills."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"POL-05","stem":"Pluralism in politics argues that power in liberal democracies is:","options":{"A":"Concentrated in the hands of a small ruling elite.","B":"Held exclusively by elected politicians.","C":"Distributed among many competing groups","D":"Shared equally among all citizens through direct democracy."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-POL-05","scaffold":"Pluralism (Dahl): multiple competing centres of power; open political system; government reflects competing pressures. Contrasts with: Elitism (C. Wright Mills: power elite of military, corporate, political leaders dominates), Marxism (state serves ruling class/capitalist interests), New Right (state itself is the problem — too powerful). Edexcel requires you to evaluate these theories using UK examples.","reforge":{"stem":"Which evidence would MOST effectively challenge a pluralist account of UK democracy?","options":{"A":"The existence of many different pressure groups in the UK.","B":"Evidence that wealthy corporate donors disproportionately influence party policy and that some groups (e.g. the homeless) have minimal political access — suggesting power is not evenly distributed among competing groups.","C":"The fact that elections are held every five years.","D":"The presence of a free press in the UK."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"POL-06","stem":"A student uses the same recent example (Brexit) for every question on a Politics paper. What is the examiner likely to penalise?","options":{"A":"Brexit is the most important recent event and should anchor all political examples.","B":"Any use of referendum examples is penalised in Edexcel Politics mark schemes.","C":"Students should restrict themselves to pre-2015 examples for consistency.","D":"Lack of range — Edexcel requires contemporary examples relevant to the specific question."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-TECH-01","scaffold":"Edexcel Politics examiners repeatedly flag over-reliance on Brexit, the 2019 election, or COVID-19. Top answers demonstrate range across different political periods, institutions, and contexts. For each question, ask: what is the BEST example — not the most familiar. Build a bank of examples across: Parliament, PM power, judiciary, parties, pressure groups, devolution, electoral systems, EU/international.","reforge":{"stem":"A question asks: 'Evaluate the power of the Prime Minister.' A top answer would use examples from:","options":{"A":"Only the current Prime Minister's term in office.","B":"Multiple Prime Ministers across different contexts — showing when PM power is strong (large majority, economic growth, united party) AND when it is constrained (hung parliament, backbench rebellions, economic crisis) — demonstrating genuine evaluative understanding.","C":"Only foreign policy examples where PMs have most freedom.","D":"The Monarch's constitutional relationship with the PM."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"POL-07","stem":"Which of the following best describes a 'pressure group' as distinct from a political party?","options":{"A":"Pressure groups influence specific policies without seeking elected office.","B":"Pressure groups are illegal in the UK.","C":"Political parties never use lobbying; only pressure groups do.","D":"Pressure groups only represent business interests."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-POL-07","scaffold":"Pressure groups vs parties: parties seek power (government), pressure groups seek influence. Insider groups: consulted by government, formal access (BMA on health policy). Outsider groups: excluded or choose exclusion, use direct action, media campaigns (Extinction Rebellion). Grant's distinction: sectional (self-interest — trade unions) vs cause groups (public interest — Amnesty International). AQA/Edexcel expect you to evaluate whether pressure groups enhance or threaten democracy.","reforge":{"stem":"Extinction Rebellion blockading roads is an example of an outsider pressure group tactic because:","options":{"A":"They lobby MPs directly in Westminster.","B":"They use direct action to generate media attention and public pressure rather than working within formal channels — either by choice or because they lack insider access to government.","C":"They represent a section of society rather than a cause.","D":"Their membership is too small to qualify as an insider group."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"POL-08","stem":"Evaluate the view that referendums are a threat to representative democracy in the UK. (Essay plan — 30 marks)","type":"short_answer","model_answer":"For (threat): bypass Parliament (undermines parliamentary sovereignty), can be used tactically (Cameron's Brexit gamble to manage party), simplify complex issues to binary choice (Brexit — multiple leave options collapsed), tyranny of majority can harm minorities, result may reflect campaign misinformation rather than informed choice (£350m bus claim). Against: increase legitimacy of constitutional change, direct mandate stronger than parliamentary vote, force politicians to engage public on major issues (AV 2011, Scottish Independence 2014), turnout often higher than elections. Evaluation: context matters — referendums on constitutional questions (devolution, Brexit) have stronger justification than use as party management tools. UK's uncodified constitution means no formal rule on when to hold them — this is the deeper problem. Judgement: they are not inherently a threat but depend on how and why they are called — executive discretion over timing and framing is the real risk.","tag":["MC-POL-03","MC-TECH-02"],"scaffold":"30-mark Edexcel essays: four developed points minimum, each with knowledge, example, analysis, and evaluation. AO3 = most marks — reach a substantiated judgement that directly answers 'threat or not' with conditions."}]},"POL-2":{"label":"Paper 2: UK Government & Branches","color":"#1e3a5f","questions":[{"id":"POL-09","stem":"The Human Rights Act 1998 incorporated the European Convention on Human Rights into UK law. Which statement correctly describes its effect on parliamentary sovereignty?","options":{"A":"It allows UK courts to strike down Acts of Parliament that violate Convention rights.","B":"It removed Parliament's right to legislate on human rights matters.","C":"It transferred human rights jurisdiction entirely to the European Court of Human Rights.","D":"Courts can issue a 'declaration of incompatibility' if legislation breaches the Convention, but Parliament retains the sovereign right to change or not change the law."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-POL-05","scaffold":"HRA 1998: Section 4 allows courts to declare legislation incompatible with the ECHR — but crucially, this does NOT strike down the law. Parliament remains sovereign and can choose to amend, repeal, or ignore the declaration. This is a key difference from the USA, where the Supreme Court can strike down unconstitutional legislation. The HRA is a constitutional statute (AXA General Insurance v Lord Advocate) but not entrenched.","reforge":{"stem":"A UK court issues a declaration of incompatibility under the HRA. What must the government do?","options":{"A":"Immediately repeal the offending legislation.","B":"Take the case to the European Court of Human Rights.","C":"Nothing — a declaration of incompatibility has no legal binding effect on Parliament; the government may choose to amend the law, but is not legally required to.","D":"Suspend the legislation until Parliament votes."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"POL-10","stem":"Insider pressure groups differ from outsider groups primarily in that:","options":{"A":"Insiders have regular, formal access to government ministers and civil servants and are consulted during policy-making, while outsiders are excluded from this process.","B":"Insiders have more members and larger budgets.","C":"Outsiders use only illegal protest methods.","D":"Insiders only operate at local government level."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-POL-06","scaffold":"Grant's insider/outsider distinction: Insiders (e.g. BMA, CBI, NFU) are consulted by government, may sit on advisory committees, have direct access to ministers. Outsiders (e.g. Greenpeace, Fathers4Justice) lack this access — either by choice (ideological outsiders like Amnesty UK) or by exclusion (outsiders by necessity). Insider status gives greater influence on policy detail; outsiders rely on public opinion and media pressure. The distinction is not about legality.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a pressure group choose to remain an outsider even if offered insider status?","options":{"A":"Insider groups receive less media coverage.","B":"Some groups prioritise ideological independence over influence — accepting insider status might require moderating demands or endorsing government policy they oppose, compromising their campaigning identity.","C":"Outsider groups have legally protected status that insiders lose.","D":"Government only consults insider groups on trivial matters."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"POL-11","stem":"Judicial independence in the UK is protected by which mechanism?","options":{"A":"Senior judges hold office 'during good behaviour' and can only be removed by a vote of both Houses of Parliament, insulating them from executive pressure.","B":"Judges are elected by the public every five years.","C":"The Lord Chancellor personally approves all judicial decisions.","D":"Judges are appointed by a cross-party parliamentary committee with a veto."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-POL-07","scaffold":"Constitutional Reform Act 2005 strengthened judicial independence: (1) Created the Supreme Court (replacing Law Lords in Parliament); (2) Created Judicial Appointments Commission (independent of Lord Chancellor); (3) Lord Chancellor's role reformed. Senior judges cannot be dismissed by the executive — they hold office 'during good behaviour' and removal requires an address of both Houses. Security of tenure means judges can make unpopular rulings without fear of dismissal.","reforge":{"stem":"Why was the creation of the Supreme Court (2005) significant for judicial independence?","options":{"A":"It gave the Supreme Court the power to strike down Acts of Parliament.","B":"It removed the Law Lords from the House of Lords, ending the fusion of judicial and legislative functions — judges no longer sat in Parliament, creating clearer separation of powers and reinforcing the appearance and reality of judicial independence.","C":"It allowed judges to review government spending decisions.","D":"It transferred all appeals from UK courts to the Supreme Court."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"POL-12","stem":"Which of the following is the strongest argument that pressure groups enhance democracy?","options":{"A":"They replace political parties as the main vehicle for political participation.","B":"Large pressure groups always reflect majority public opinion.","C":"They provide a continuous channel for citizen participation between elections, represent specific interests that parties may ignore, and hold government accountable through scrutiny and publicity.","D":"They are subject to democratic elections by their members."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-POL-08","scaffold":"Pro-democracy arguments: (1) Participation: elections every 5 years are insufficient; pressure groups allow ongoing engagement; (2) Representation: parties must appeal to majorities — pressure groups give voice to minorities and specific interests; (3) Accountability: scrutinise government, expose failures (e.g. Liberty on civil liberties); (4) Information: provide expertise to government. Counter: some groups represent narrow interests, lack internal democracy, and wealthy groups have disproportionate access.","reforge":{"stem":"Which argument MOST effectively challenges the view that pressure groups enhance democracy?","options":{"A":"Pressure groups often have more members than political parties.","B":"Insider groups with financial resources have disproportionate access to government compared to poorly-funded groups representing less wealthy interests — this may reinforce rather than correct existing inequalities of power.","C":"Pressure groups sometimes use illegal protest tactics.","D":"The government is not legally required to listen to pressure groups."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"POL-13","stem":"The doctrine of judicial neutrality requires that judges:","options":{"A":"Always rule in favour of the government in constitutional cases.","B":"Apply the law as written without allowing personal, political, or moral views to influence their decisions.","C":"Consult the public before issuing rulings on controversial matters.","D":"Refer all politically sensitive cases to Parliament for decision."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-POL-09","scaffold":"Judicial neutrality: judges are supposed to apply the law impartially, without political bias. BUT: critics (Griffith, 'The Politics of the Judiciary') argue senior judges are drawn from a narrow social elite (white, male, public school, Oxbridge) which shapes their outlook. Cases involving trade unions, immigration, or civil liberties show a pattern of conservative rulings. Judicial review is inherently political — deciding whether the executive has acted lawfully involves value judgements.","reforge":{"stem":"Griffith argued that judicial neutrality is a myth. What was his main evidence?","options":{"A":"Judges openly campaign for political parties and publicly declare their views.","B":"Senior judges were disproportionately from elite, white, male, Oxbridge backgrounds — critics argue this produces unconscious bias in interpretation of the law.","C":"Government ministers have powers to direct individual judges on how they should rule.","D":"Judges consistently refused to apply the Human Rights Act after its introduction in 1998."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"POL-14","stem":"A student argues that the UK now has a codified constitution in all but name. Which evidence BEST supports this?","options":{"A":"The UK has joined the European Convention on Human Rights.","B":"Parliament passes laws using a simple majority like any other legislation.","C":"The Prime Minister has a fixed term of four years.","D":"A growing body of constitutional statutes (HRA, Constitutional Reform Act, Fixed-term Parliaments Act) are treated by courts as higher law, and constitutional conventions increasingly constrain government behaviour — creating de facto constitutional limits."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-POL-10","scaffold":"The 'quasi-codified' argument: (1) Constitutional statutes (Thoburn v Sunderland): courts treat certain Acts (ECA 1972, HRA 1998, etc.) as superior to ordinary legislation — they cannot be impliedly repealed; (2) Supreme Court's Miller judgments (2017, 2019) asserted courts can review prerogative powers; (3) Conventions increasingly formalised. Counter-argument: Parliament can still repeal any statute; the FTPA was repealed in 2022; no formal entrenchment exists.","reforge":{"stem":"The Fixed-term Parliaments Act 2011 was repealed in 2022. What does this reveal about the UK constitution?","options":{"A":"Constitutional statutes are fully entrenched and cannot be repealed.","B":"That despite the 'constitutional statute' doctrine, Parliament retains ultimate sovereignty — any Act, however significant, can be repealed by a simple majority, confirming the constitution remains fundamentally uncodified and flexible.","C":"The government acted unconstitutionally in repealing it.","D":"The Supreme Court should have prevented the repeal."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"POL-15","stem":"Which case demonstrated most dramatically that UK courts could scrutinise the exercise of royal prerogative?","options":{"A":"Factortame (1990)","B":"R (Miller) v Prime Minister [2019] — the Supreme Court ruled the prorogation of Parliament for five weeks was unlawful.","C":"AXA General Insurance v Lord Advocate (2011)","D":"Entick v Carrington (1765)"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-POL-11","scaffold":"Miller 2 (2019): Boris Johnson prorogued Parliament for 5 weeks during the Brexit crisis. The Supreme Court ruled unanimously that prorogation was unlawful — it had the effect of frustrating Parliament's ability to carry out its constitutional functions without reasonable justification. Parliament was recalled immediately. Significant: the court asserted it could review prerogative powers that had previously been considered non-justiciable — a major expansion of judicial review.","reforge":{"stem":"Why was Miller 2 (2019) controversial among those who support parliamentary sovereignty?","options":{"A":"It allowed the EU to override the UK Parliament's decision on Brexit.","B":"Critics argued the Court overstepped its role by reviewing a political decision — defenders said it correctly upheld the constitutional principle that Parliament must authorise triggering Article 50.","C":"The ruling prevented Brexit from occurring by blocking the referendum result.","D":"It permanently removed the royal prerogative to ratify international treaties."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"POL-16","stem":"The Salisbury Convention dictates that the House of Lords should not:","options":{"A":"Delay money bills for more than one month.","B":"Reject at second or third reading any government bill that was included in the governing party's manifesto.","C":"Appoint more than 800 life peers in total.","D":"Amend legislation already passed by the House of Commons."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-POL-12","scaffold":"Salisbury Convention (1945): after Labour's landslide, the Conservative-dominated Lords agreed not to wreck manifesto commitments the electorate had endorsed — the Lords would give government bills a second reading even if they opposed them. Distinct from the Parliament Acts (which give the Commons power to pass bills without the Lords after delay). The convention is unwritten and was tested by the Lords' resistance to the Hunting Act 2004 and welfare reform bills.","reforge":{"stem":"How does the Salisbury Convention limit the democratic legitimacy argument for Lords reform?","options":{"A":"The Salisbury Convention proves the Lords already behave democratically in all votes.","B":"Because the Lords follow the convention and don't block manifesto bills, reformers argue democratic reform isn't urgent — the informal check is sufficient.","C":"The Salisbury Convention was formally written into statute law in 2005.","D":"The Lords have never broken the Salisbury Convention in its entire history."},"correct":"B"}}]}});
